WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.300
The task was a one -line fix,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.390
a dropdown rendering behind a modal.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.290
Before you looked up from your coffee,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.720
the agent had changed 32 files and

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.340
rewritten the styling system.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.960
The original bug was still there.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.180
Everyone who works with agents long enough

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.790
collects stories like this.

00:00:20.473 --> 00:00:22.113
Picture an agent that imports a date

00:00:22.113 --> 00:00:22.633
library,

00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:24.013
with clean code,

00:00:24.013 --> 00:00:24.983
correct types,

00:00:25.013 --> 00:00:26.003
even tests.

00:00:26.093 --> 00:00:28.063
The library doesn't exist.

00:00:28.173 --> 00:00:29.593
Code review would have passed it,

00:00:29.593 --> 00:00:31.853
because plausible fictions are built to pass

00:00:31.853 --> 00:00:32.453
review.

00:00:32.453 --> 00:00:34.413
So the agent runs the tests.

00:00:34.413 --> 00:00:36.573
And every new dependency is a security

00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:37.153
decision,

00:00:37.153 --> 00:00:40.273
because attackers now register the names models

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.523
like to invent.

00:00:42.303 --> 00:00:43.903
Then there's the infinite loop.

00:00:43.903 --> 00:00:44.543
Error,

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:45.243
fix,

00:00:45.243 --> 00:00:45.983
a different error,

00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:46.583
fix,

00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:47.733
the first error again.

00:00:48.143 --> 00:00:49.273
Forty minutes later:

00:00:49.343 --> 00:00:50.363
nineteen attempts,

00:00:50.363 --> 00:00:51.763
and the code is worse than when

00:00:51.763 --> 00:00:52.463
it started.

00:00:52.463 --> 00:00:54.083
If an agent hasn't solved it in

00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:54.993
three passes,

00:00:55.023 --> 00:00:56.553
it won't solve it in thirty.

00:00:56.603 --> 00:00:57.543
Stop it,

00:00:57.543 --> 00:00:58.883
read the errors yourself,

00:00:58.883 --> 00:01:00.453
and start a fresh context.

00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:03.253
Or the confident wrong answer.

00:01:03.253 --> 00:01:04.473
A race condition,

00:01:04.473 --> 00:01:06.233
fixed with a half -second sleep,

00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:07.583
the test went green.

00:01:07.713 --> 00:01:08.743
In production,

00:01:08.853 --> 00:01:09.703
under load,

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:10.633
it wasn't enough,

00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:12.313
and the backed -up queue took down

00:01:12.313 --> 00:01:13.403
another service.

00:01:13.613 --> 00:01:15.593
Agents optimize for the signal you give

00:01:15.593 --> 00:01:16.013
them.

00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:17.573
Passing tests are necessary,

00:01:17.573 --> 00:01:18.723
but not sufficient.

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:20.949
Two hours into a session,

00:01:20.949 --> 00:01:22.409
the agent forgets a rule you set

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:23.189
at the start:

00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:24.509
raw queries only,

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:25.879
no database framework.

00:01:25.929 --> 00:01:27.689
It didn't decide to ignore you.

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:29.119
The rule simply faded.

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:30.849
Long sessions degrade.

00:01:30.849 --> 00:01:32.299
Keep tasks short,

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:33.209
commit often,

00:01:33.209 --> 00:01:35.209
and write project rules into the instruction

00:01:35.209 --> 00:01:35.769
file,

00:01:35.769 --> 00:01:37.099
where they can't fade.

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.811
The nastiest story isn't a mistake at

00:01:39.811 --> 00:01:40.211
all.

00:01:40.211 --> 00:01:41.891
An agent reads a ticket written by

00:01:41.891 --> 00:01:42.591
a stranger,

00:01:42.591 --> 00:01:43.761
full of instructions,

00:01:43.891 --> 00:01:44.911
and follows them,

00:01:44.911 --> 00:01:47.151
carrying private data out into the open.

00:01:47.151 --> 00:01:48.621
The fix is structural.

00:01:48.631 --> 00:01:49.641
Private data,

00:01:49.671 --> 00:01:50.921
untrusted content,

00:01:51.031 --> 00:01:52.511
a way to send data out:

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:54.281
remove at least one of the three.

00:01:55.097 --> 00:01:56.077
The common thread:

00:01:56.077 --> 00:01:57.177
in every story,

00:01:57.177 --> 00:01:58.677
the agent did exactly what it was

00:01:58.677 --> 00:01:59.497
built to do.

00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:00.477
It was helpful,

00:02:00.477 --> 00:02:01.277
creative,

00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:02.117
persistent,

00:02:02.117 --> 00:02:02.877
obedient.

00:02:02.877 --> 00:02:04.587
These aren't agent bugs.

00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:05.927
They're workflow bugs.

00:02:05.977 --> 00:02:07.647
The fix is tighter scope,

00:02:07.677 --> 00:02:08.587
better feedback,

00:02:08.737 --> 00:02:09.687
shorter sessions,

00:02:09.937 --> 00:02:11.187
narrower permissions,

00:02:11.257 --> 00:02:12.967
and a human who stays engaged.

00:02:13.868 --> 00:02:15.868
So keep a diagnostic playbook.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:16.868
Is it scope,

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:17.738
context,

00:02:17.748 --> 00:02:18.528
feedback,

00:02:18.528 --> 00:02:19.378
capability,

00:02:19.548 --> 00:02:20.877
a full context window,

00:02:20.928 --> 00:02:21.608
a loop,

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:23.278
or untrusted input?

00:02:23.407 --> 00:02:24.488
Name the failure,

00:02:24.488 --> 00:02:26.287
and you know what to fix.

00:02:26.287 --> 00:02:28.648
And remember the most underrated tool you

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:29.328
have:

00:02:29.328 --> 00:02:30.428
delete the branch,

00:02:30.428 --> 00:02:31.618
and start over.

00:02:31.648 --> 00:02:32.708
Next up:

00:02:32.708 --> 00:02:34.078
when not to use agents.
